.TH DIRK 1 "2026-09-07" "dirk 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
dirk \- a terminal multiplexer that knows what its sessions are for
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dirk
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.RI [ COMMAND ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dirk
runs a terminal multiplexer with a clickable project tree, static layouts, and
workspaces named from what the program inside them says it is doing.
.PP
With no command it attaches to the session, starting it if it is not running.
The session outlives the terminal it was started from: close that terminal and
everything in it keeps going.
.PP
Where
.BR tmux (1)
gives you panes and asks you to remember what is in them,
.B dirk
starts from the other end. The sidebar is the product and the panes hang off
it: boards at the top, the work in the middle, and between them whatever is
interrupting.
.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Running a session
.TP
.B attach
Attach to the session, starting it if it is not running. This is the default.
.TP
.B server
Be the session. Started for you; not usually typed.
.TP
.B relay
A session on standard input and output. What
.B \-\-remote
runs on the far side.
.
.SS Asking a running session
From a shell or from inside a pane. Answers are JSON, including the failures,
because the caller is a program and prose on standard error is not something a
program can branch on. Ids are opaque and stable:
.B w7
is a workspace and
.B w7:p12
a pane in it. Every managed pane is told its own, so
.B \-\-current
works from inside one without looking anything up.
.TP
.B workspace \fRlist | focus | create | rename | close
.TP
.B pane \fRlist | focus | split | read | run | send\-text | send\-keys |
.B wait\-output | attach | close
.TP
.B layout \fRlist | open
.TP
.B agent \fRlist | rules | explain | start | state | prompt | wait | hooks
.TP
.B api \fRschema
.TP
.B completion \fRbash | zsh | fish
.TP
.B session \fRinfo | list | reload | commands | notify
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-session " NAME"
Which session to attach to. Defaults to
.BR default .
.TP
.BI \-\-remote " TARGET"
Attach to a session on
.IR TARGET ,
over
.BR ssh (1).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-session
One process, no session. Ends with this terminal, which is what
.B dirk
was before it had a server.
.TP
.B \-\-skill
Print what a coding agent needs in order to drive a session. Generated from the
command table, so it cannot describe a surface that no longer exists.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.
.SH KEYS
The prefix is
.BR Ctrl\-Space .
.BR Ctrl\-a " and " Ctrl\-b
are both load\-bearing in every shell line editor; Ctrl\-Space is NUL, which
nothing sends on purpose. Press it twice to send a literal one through.
.PP
After the prefix:
.TS
tab(@);
lB l.
n@New workspace in this project
o@Open a project
x@Close the focused pane
| v@Split into columns
\- s@Split into rows
;@Next pane in this workspace
Tab j k@Next / previous workspace
1 2 3@Jump to a board
b@Hide the nav
u@Release a held name, so naming may claim the workspace again
r@Restart a stopped pane
w@Give the nav the keyboard: j k to move, Enter to go, Escape back
a@Start an agent here
[@Read this pane's scrollback, and copy out of it
/@Find a line, in any pane
d@Detach: leave, and let everything keep running
q@Quit: end every shell and every agent
.TE
.PP
In the scrollback view: the wheel,
.BR PgUp / PgDn ,
.B g
and
.B G
move through it;
.BR h " " j " " k " " l ,
the arrows,
.B 0
and
.B $
move the cursor;
.B v
starts a selection;
.B y
or
.B Enter
copies and leaves;
.B q
or
.B Esc
leaves without copying. Dragging with the pointer selects and copies in one
gesture. A pane whose program asked for mouse events keeps them.
.PP
Leaving and quitting are different things. A session outlives the terminal it
was started in, so detaching is the ordinary way out. Quitting ends the work.
.PP
.B Esc
never leaves. It closes the picker, or hands the keyboard back from the nav,
and does nothing else. Everything else goes straight through to the program in
the pane.
.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B dirk
runs with no configuration file.
.I ~/.config/dirk/config.toml
overrides what it names and leaves the rest alone: who the bar says you are,
where the project picker looks, the sidebar width, the scrollback depth, the
shell, the boards, the layouts, the naming thresholds, and the size a session
has when nothing is attached to it
.RB ( [server] " " headless_cols ", " headless_rows ).
.PP
.B [identity]
decides the first cells of the bar:
.B name
(empty means
.BR $USER ),
.B mark
(empty means the glyph set's),
.B session
.RI ( never " | " named " | " always )
and
.B host
.RI ( never " | " remote " | " always ).
.B remote
means dirk is on the far side of an
.BR ssh (1)
connection, which is worth saying: a local session and one on another machine
are otherwise identical on screen.
.PP
.B dirk session reload
re\-reads it without restarting. An open layout keeps its panes.
.PP
The full reference, with defaults, is in the README shipped alongside this page
in
.IR /usr/local/share/doc/dirk .
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DIRK_SSH
The program
.B \-\-remote
reaches the far side with.
.TP
.B DIRK_REMOTE
The far\-side
.B dirk
for
.BR \-\-remote .
Defaults to
.BR dirk .
.TP
.B DIRK_DEBUG
A file for a session's own standard error to land in.
.TP
.B SHELL
What a pane runs when the configuration does not name a shell.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/dirk/agents/NAME.toml
Detection rules for one harness, replacing the ones
.B dirk
ships under that name.
.TP
.I ~/.config/dirk/config.toml
The configuration file, if it exists.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B dirk
exits 0 on success. A command asking a session something exits non\-zero when
the session refuses or cannot be reached, and prints the reason as JSON on
standard output.
.
